  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What are the 8 key questions on the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "The 8 key questions are a set of critical questions inside the Wolverhampton PHV knowledge test that you must answer correctly to pass. They cover safeguarding, plying for hire, and disability awareness. Getting even one key question wrong means you fail the whole test, no matter how high your overall score is.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What happens if you get a key question wrong?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "You fail the test automatically. Even if you score 27 out of 28 overall, one wrong key question means the paper fails. The pass rule has two parts: you must score at least 21 out of 28 AND answer all 8 key questions correctly.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How many questions are on the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "The test has 28 questions and you have 45 minutes to complete it. To pass, you need at least 21 correct answers (75%) and all 8 key questions correct.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How many attempts do you get at the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "You get a limited number of attempts — three. If you fail all three, you must wait 12 months before you can try again, and each resit costs money. That is why preparing properly before your first attempt matters so much.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How do I prepare for the key questions?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Focus your revision on the key question topics — safeguarding, plying for hire, and disability awareness — until your answers are automatic. Practise under timed exam conditions with 28 questions in 45 minutes, review every question you get wrong, and keep repeating the key topics until you stop making mistakes in them.",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What Are the 8 Key Questions? — Wolverhampton PHV Test"
  description="What are the 8 key questions on the Wolverhampton PHV test? Get one wrong and you fail. Learn what they cover and how to prepare for your taxi badge test."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>What Are the 8 Key Questions on the Wolverhampton PHV Test?</h1>

      <p>
        If you are preparing for the Wolverhampton PHV test (the "taxi badge"
        knowledge test), the <strong>8 key questions</strong> are the single
        most important thing to understand. Here is the rule that catches
        drivers out: <strong>if you get even one key question wrong, you fail
        the whole test</strong> — no matter how good your overall score is.
        You could answer 27 out of 28 questions correctly and still fail,
        because the one you missed was a key question.
      </p>

      <p>
        This page explains what the key questions are, what topics they cover,
        and how to prepare for them, in plain English.
      </p>

      <h2>What happens if you get a key question wrong?</h2>

      <p>
        You fail automatically. The pass rule for the Wolverhampton PHV test
        has <strong>two parts</strong>, and you must meet both:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Score at least <strong>21 out of 28</strong> overall (75%),
          <strong>and</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          Answer <strong>all 8 key questions correctly</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Miss either one and the paper fails. This is why two drivers with the
        same overall score can get different results: one passed all the key
        questions, the other slipped up on just one of them.
      </p>

      <h2>How many questions are on the Wolverhampton PHV test?</h2>

      <p>
        The test has <strong>28 questions</strong> and you have
        <strong>45 minutes</strong> to complete it. That is plenty of time if
        you know the material — the danger is not the clock, it is rushing and
        misreading a key question. Read every question twice before you
        answer.
      </p>

      <h2>What topics do the 8 key questions cover?</h2>

      <p>
        The key questions focus on the areas where a driver's mistake could
        put a passenger at risk. They cover:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Safeguarding</strong> — recognising and responding to signs
          that a child or vulnerable adult may be at risk, and knowing what a
          driver must do about it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plying for hire</strong> — the legal difference between a
          private hire vehicle and a hackney carriage, and why a PHV driver
          must never pick up passengers who have not pre-booked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Disability awareness</strong> — a driver's legal duties
          towards disabled passengers, including assistance dogs and
          wheelchair users, under equality law.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These are not trick questions. The council treats them as
        non-negotiable because they are about passenger safety and the law —
        the things a licensed driver simply must know. The rest of the test
        draws on the wider syllabus, including customer care, licensing and
        compliance, vehicle and road safety, and law and regulations.
      </p>

      <h2>How many attempts do you get?</h2>

      <p>
        The stakes are higher than many drivers realise. You get
        <!-- VERIFY: 3 attempts and 12-month wait — confirm against the Council source PDFs -->
        <strong>three attempts</strong> at the test. Fail all three and you
        must wait <strong>12 months</strong> before you can try again — a
        whole year without your badge.
      </p>

      <p>
        Resits are not free either. Each attempt costs money
        <!-- VERIFY: exact fees (~£15 test, ~£40–50 course) — confirm against the Council source PDFs -->
        (the test fee is around £15, and you may also need to pay for a
        knowledge course, typically £40–50). Between fees and lost time, the
        cheapest way through is to pass first time.
      </p>

      <h2>How do I prepare for the key questions?</h2>

      <p>
        Three things work, and they work together:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Drill the key topics until they are automatic.</strong>
          Safeguarding, plying for hire, and disability awareness are a small
          part of the syllabus, but they decide your result. Keep practising
          them until you stop getting any wrong — not "mostly right", but
          every time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practise under real exam conditions.</strong> Take timed
          mock tests with 28 questions in 45 minutes, marked the same way as
          the real thing — 21 correct plus all 8 key questions. If your mock
          passes are relying on a high overall score while you still drop key
          questions, you are not ready yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learn from every mistake.</strong> Do not just note that an
          answer was wrong — understand why the correct answer is correct.
          Wrong answers you have understood rarely come back to bite you on
          test day.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If you want a structured way to do this, the
        <a
          href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/phv-prep-uk-taxi-badge-test/id6776364932"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">PHV Prep UK app</a
        >
        lets you drill the key questions until they're automatic, with timed
        mock exams scored exactly like the real test and a plain-English AI
        tutor for anything you don't understand.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>

      <p>
        The key questions are one part of a wider preparation plan — see our
        full guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass"
          >how to pass the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >. You can also learn more about the app on the
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk">PHV Prep UK page</a>, get help via
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/support">support</a>, or read our
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>PHV Prep UK is an independent study aid.</strong> It is not
        affiliated with, authorised by, or endorsed by City of Wolverhampton
        Council or any licensing authority. Always check the council's
        official guidance for the latest test requirements and fees.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
